EDMA v1 TX tuple ownership host fixture
=====================================

Only patch 014 and this new test are covered. No make, target/module/firmware
build, device access or performance measurement is performed. The runner
uses a supplied local Git cache, exact d8f802f08fd8ff31057ba58edb20bbe448e7b505,
and freshly replays current patches 001-013 plus 014. Existing audit programs
are neither imported nor executed. A new EDMA-TX-* output directory is
required; all failed preparation, compile and run evidence is retained.

Example commands, from the source checkout:

/usr/bin/python3 tests/test-edma-tx-slot-ownership.py --upstream-cache /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/upstreams/nss/qca-nss-dp --output /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/audits/20261030/core-risk-phase2/EDMA-TX-gcc-final --compiler /opt/homebrew/bin/gcc-16
/usr/bin/python3 tests/test-edma-tx-slot-ownership.py --upstream-cache /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/upstreams/nss/qca-nss-dp --output /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/audits/20261030/core-risk-phase2/EDMA-TX-clang-final --compiler /usr/bin/clang --sanitize

Extraction and validation boundary
----------------------------------
The complete original edma_ring_xmit, edma_clean_tx, edma_tx_slot_take,
edma_if_xmit wrapper, TXDESC/TXCMPL setup/cleanup and edma_configure_rings
bodies are extracted with comment/literal-aware balanced-brace parsing.
Actual architecture, descriptor/register and dataplane headers are included
with only their kernel include directives removed. Original fragment and
prepared-file hashes, exact replay logs and source-site inventory are saved.
All other C function bodies in touched source files must remain unchanged;
existing descriptor/preheader/ring declarations and parameter macros are
also checked. This is not a target compiler or full kernel include proof.

Deterministic condition-variable hooks are inserted immediately after old
skb-clear / DMA-capture operations and after the new full tuple capture.
The rest of each extracted function is unchanged. Real pthread mutexes model
spin_lock_bh mutual exclusion, not kernel preemption, IRQ or RCU semantics.
Successful baseline/candidate mapping, descriptor publication and retirement
traces must match. Both old race schedules must fail the precise DMA ledger.
New schedules also pause old retirement after detach, permit same-index reuse,
then require each mapping to be unmapped by its own original local tuple.

The ledger checks map device, direction, full dma_addr_t and exact map size;
free requires the skb's map already retired. Successful DMA address zero is
tested in normal completion and TXDESC cleanup. A synthetic >32-bit mapping
tests host metadata preservation only, not EDMA hardware address capability.
Other cases include wrap, occupied cross-ring indices, invalid/empty opaque,
all producer returns, padding consumption, mapping error, timestamp position,
wrapper busy/drop/expand paths, foreign ring cleanup and indirect TXCMPL
teardown. Named source mutants must compile cleanly and fail their intended
semantic stderr gate. Positive compile output and candidate stderr must be
empty. Clang ASan/UBSan canaries must emit the expected positive diagnostics;
UBSAN_OPTIONS includes halt_on_error=1. Host LSan is disabled; explicit skb,
streaming-map and coherent-allocation ledgers check fixture resource balance.

Production protocol and limits
-----------------------------
One global slot lock replaces the per-ring producer lock at the same producer
critical-section boundaries. It also protects tuple take in completion and
TXDESC teardown. After take, unmap/free uses only the local tuple outside the
lock; no retire code returns to clear shared DMA fields. DMA zero is not an
empty marker. Ring owner and exact map length are host-only metadata.

The old per-ring tx_lock field and its setup initialization are intentionally
retained, but are no longer used by the TX producer. Removing them would be
unrelated layout churn. Stock has one TX ring, so producer-to-producer
serialization is unchanged in scope. Completion now shares the lock and can
wait for the existing padding/PTP/map/doorbell producer section. Synthetic
multi-ring producers serialize globally because the opaque namespace is
global. No latency, throughput, contention or fairness result is claimed.

edma_configure_rings initializes the lock/empty tuple array only under a
PRE-LIVE caller assumption. Calling that memset or spin_lock_init with live
slots/readers is NOT safe reinitialization. The fixture invokes it only
before publication; it does not certify every real module-init/error/reset
lifetime. edma_hw_init caller quiescence and all lifecycle repair remain
separate acceptance boundaries.

Teardown selects only its original TXDESC consumer-to-producer range and
original TXCMPL completion range. A recorded foreign ring owner is skipped;
there is no all-slot force sweep. This patch does not establish DMA idle,
stop/disable acknowledgement, visibility of an in-flight completion, FW
handoff safety, generation/duplicate-opaque protection, or concurrent
completion-ring consumer/MMIO/free safety. Tests release modeled hardware
ownership before teardown; that is an input assumption, not hardware proof.
Probe/NAPI/active membership, NSS/SSDK and asynchronous PHY ownership remain
outside this patch. Never use passing host checks as a firmware fault cause.
